{
 "title": "How 2.3 Million Blocks Became the Great Pyramid #Shorts",
 "description": "How the Great Pyramid of Giza went up for Pharaoh Khufu around 2560 BCE: about 2.3 million blocks averaging about 2.5 tonnes, most of the limestone quarried at Giza itself, fine white Tura limestone for the casing and granite brought from Aswan by Nile boats. It stood about 146 m tall and is about 139 m today. The ramp shown is one theory; how the ramps worked is still debated.\nSources: UNESCO World Heritage Centre (Memphis and its Necropolis, the Pyramid Fields from Giza to Dahshur); Ancient Egypt Research Associates (the Giza workers' town).\n\nVisuals and voice are AI-assisted.",
